N,R,S="N","R","S"
SCENES={
"title":[(N,"Operation Gate. The Tower of Vectors. Linear algebra for GATE Data Science and A I, twenty twenty seven."),
(N,"Nine floors, nine twists. Ramu asks why. Somu answers with everyday things. Let us climb.")],
"vs":[(N,"Floor one. Vector spaces."),
(R,"Somu, what is a vector space? Why do people not just say, a list of numbers?"),
(S,"Because the rules matter. You must be able to add two vectors and scale one, and never fall out of the room. A room like that is a vector space."),
(R,"So is every flat sheet of paper a vector space?"),
(S,"Only if it passes through the origin. The plane x plus y plus z equals zero contains the zero vector, so it is a subspace. The line y equals x plus one misses the origin, so it is not."),
(S,"Span is everything you can build from some vectors. A basis is the smallest set that still builds the whole room. The number of vectors in a basis is the dimension."),
(R,"Example, please."),
(S,"One comma zero, and one comma one, build every point of the plane. So they are a basis, and the dimension is two. One comma two and two comma four lie on the same line, so they are dependent."),
(N,"Twist one. The space of two by two matrices has dimension four. The symmetric ones have dimension three. And polynomials of degree at most two also have dimension three. Even matrices and polynomials are vectors.")],
"mt":[(N,"Floor two. Matrix types."),
(R,"There are so many names. Symmetric, orthogonal, idempotent. Why?"),
(S,"Because each type promises a behaviour, and GATE asks you to use the promise. Symmetric means A equals A transpose. Then the eigenvalues are real and the eigenvectors are perpendicular."),
(S,"Orthogonal means Q transpose times Q is the identity. Rotations are like that. They keep lengths and angles. Triangular matrices have their eigenvalues sitting on the diagonal."),
(R,"And the determinant and the trace?"),
(S,"Determinant is the volume scale factor. Trace is the sum of the diagonal. And the determinant of A B is the determinant of A times the determinant of B. But the transpose of A B is B transpose times A transpose. The order flips."),
(S,"Idempotent means A squared equals A, like a projection. Nilpotent means some power is zero. Positive definite is the royal family. We meet them again on floor six."),
(N,"Twist two. A rotation by ninety degrees, zero, minus one, one, zero, is a real matrix, but it has no real eigenvalue. Nothing keeps its direction when you rotate by ninety degrees. Its eigenvalues are i and minus i.")],
"ls":[(N,"Floor three. Linear systems."),
(R,"Why do we even solve A x equals b by hand? A computer can do it."),
(S,"Because GATE asks how many solutions there are, not just what they are. Gaussian elimination turns the system into a staircase. Then you read the answer from the bottom."),
(S,"Take x plus y plus z equals six, two y plus five z equals minus four, two x plus five y minus z equals twenty seven. Eliminate, and the staircase gives z equals minus two, y equals three, x equals five."),
(R,"Check it."),
(S,"Five plus three minus two is six. Six minus ten is minus four. Ten plus fifteen plus two is twenty seven. All three agree."),
(S,"The rule: rank of A equals rank of A augmented with b means a solution exists. If it also equals the number of unknowns, it is unique. If it is smaller, there are infinitely many."),
(N,"Twist three. x plus y equals two and two x plus two y equals four are the same line twice. Rank one, two unknowns, so infinite solutions. But change the right side to five, and the rank jumps, and the system has no solution at all.")],
"rn":[(N,"Floor four. Rank and nullity."),
(R,"Somu, what is rank, in one sentence?"),
(S,"The number of truly independent rows. Row two of this matrix is two times row one, so it adds nothing new. Rank is two, not three."),
(S,"Nullity counts the vectors that A sends to zero. The rank nullity theorem says rank plus nullity equals the number of columns. Two plus one equals three."),
(R,"Can you show me the vector that dies?"),
(S,"Solve x plus two y plus three z equals zero, and x plus z equals zero. You get minus one, minus one, one. Multiply it by A and everything becomes zero. That is the null space, a line."),
(S,"Rank is also the dimension of the column space. Row rank always equals column rank."),
(N,"Twist four. A three by four matrix can never have rank above three, so its null space always has dimension at least one. A wide matrix always hides a vector that vanishes.")],
"ev":[(N,"Floor five. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors."),
(R,"Why would a matrix ever keep a direction?"),
(S,"Think of a stretching rubber sheet. Most arrows change direction. A few special arrows only get longer or shorter. Those are eigenvectors, and the stretch factor is the eigenvalue."),
(S,"Take A equals four, one, two, three. The vector one comma one goes to five comma five, so the eigenvalue is five. The vector one comma minus two goes to two comma minus four, so the eigenvalue is two."),
(R,"Is there a shortcut to find them?"),
(S,"Yes. The sum of eigenvalues equals the trace, and their product equals the determinant. Five plus two is seven, which is four plus three. Five times two is ten, which is twelve minus two."),
(S,"If A has a full set of eigenvectors, A is P D P inverse. Then A to the power n is P times D to the n times P inverse. You only power the diagonal."),
(N,"Twist five. A to the fifth power has trace three thousand one hundred fifty seven, which is five to the fifth plus two to the fifth. Nobody multiplied the matrix five times. Eigenvalues did the work.")],
"qf":[(N,"Floor six. Quadratic forms."),
(R,"What is a quadratic form, and why does it matter?"),
(S,"It is x transpose A x, a bowl shaped formula. Optimization uses it, because a bowl has a bottom, a saddle does not. A is symmetric, and its eigenvalues decide the shape."),
(S,"All eigenvalues positive, positive definite, a proper bowl. All negative, negative definite, a hill. Mixed signs, indefinite, a saddle. Some zero, semi definite."),
(R,"Example?"),
(S,"A equals two, one, one, two has eigenvalues three and one. Positive definite. But A equals one, two, two, one has eigenvalues three and minus one. Indefinite."),
(N,"Twist six. The form x squared plus four x y plus y squared has positive numbers on the diagonal, yet at one comma minus one it equals one minus four plus one, which is minus two. Never judge a matrix by its diagonal. Judge it by its eigenvalues.")],
"lu":[(N,"Floor seven. L U decomposition."),
(R,"Why split a matrix into L and U?"),
(S,"Because solving is cheap when a matrix is triangular. Do the elimination once, store the multipliers in L, and the result is U. Then every new right side costs only two quick sweeps."),
(S,"Take two, one, one, four, three, three, eight, seven, nine. Row two minus two times row one. Row three minus four times row one, then minus three times the new row two."),
(S,"The multipliers two, four, three go below the diagonal of L. U is two, one, one, then zero, one, one, then zero, zero, two."),
(R,"How do I know it is right?"),
(S,"Multiply L by U. Row three is four times two, one, one plus three times zero, one, one plus zero, zero, two, which is eight, seven, nine. And the determinant is the diagonal product of U, two times one times two, which is four."),
(N,"Twist seven. If a zero lands in the pivot position, elimination stalls, and you need to swap rows. That is why real software computes P A equals L U.")],
"svd":[(N,"Floor eight. Singular value decomposition."),
(R,"Eigenvalues only work for square matrices. What about a rectangle?"),
(S,"That is why S V D exists. Any matrix, any shape, is U times Sigma times V transpose. Rotate, stretch along axes, rotate again. The stretch factors are the singular values."),
(S,"The squares of singular values are the eigenvalues of A transpose A. Take A equals three, two, two, then two, three, minus two. A times A transpose is seventeen, eight, eight, seventeen, with eigenvalues twenty five and nine."),
(R,"So the singular values are five and three?"),
(S,"Exactly. The rank equals the number of nonzero singular values. And the sum of squares of all entries, thirty four, equals twenty five plus nine."),
(S,"Keep only the biggest singular value, and you get the best rank one copy of the matrix. This is how images are compressed and how P C A works."),
(N,"Twist eight. Throwing away small singular values is the mathematically best way to simplify a matrix. Nothing else of the same rank comes closer.")],
"wk":[(N,"Walkthrough. Watch the code run one line at a time, with the variables on the right."),
(S,"Our system is x plus y equals three, and two x minus y equals zero. The matrix A is one, one, two, minus one. The right side b is three, zero."),
(S,"The goal of elimination is to make the number under the first pivot zero. The multiplier m is the number to kill, divided by the pivot. Two divided by one is two."),
(S,"Now the loop. Row two becomes row two minus m times row one. Column zero becomes two minus two times one, which is zero. Column one becomes minus one minus two times one, which is minus three."),
(S,"Do the same to the right side. b one becomes zero minus two times three, which is minus six. The staircase is ready."),
(R,"And then?"),
(S,"Back substitution. The last row says minus three y equals minus six, so y is two. Put y back into the first row, x plus y equals three, so x is one."),
(N,"Check. One plus two is three, and two minus two is zero. That is the whole logic of Gaussian elimination. Kill the numbers below the pivot, then solve from the bottom up."),
(S,"Now power iteration. Start with the vector one, zero. Each round, multiply by A. Then divide by the biggest entry, so the numbers never explode."),
(S,"Round one gives four, two, divided by four, so the vector is one, point five. Round two gives four point five, three point five. Round three gives four point seven seven eight. The biggest entry is creeping toward five."),
(N,"The vector keeps turning toward the one, one direction, the eigenvector, and the big number is the eigenvalue. Multiply, shrink, repeat. That is the main logic.")],
"py":[(N,"Lab one. Python for linear algebra. Real code, real output."),
(R,"Somu, can I write Gaussian elimination myself?"),
(S,"Yes, in about a dozen lines. Nested loops build the staircase, and a back substitution loop reads the answer from the bottom. Run it on our system from floor three."),
(N,"The program prints five, three, minus two. The same answer we got by hand."),
(R,"And eigenvalues in code?"),
(S,"Power iteration. Multiply a vector by A again and again, and scale it down by its biggest entry. It slides toward the dominant eigenvector, and the scale factor becomes the dominant eigenvalue."),
(N,"Twenty rounds give five, with the vector one, one. That is the heart of Google's PageRank."),
(N,"Python twist. Never compare floats with equals. After elimination you may see two point nine nine nine nine nine nine where you expect three. Round the number, or use a tolerance.")],
"dsa":[(N,"Lab two. Complexity. How expensive is a matrix algorithm?"),
(R,"How do I count the cost without a stopwatch?"),
(S,"Count the innermost operation. Three nested loops, each over n, so n cubed. Run it for n equals two, four, eight, sixteen, and you see eight, sixty four, five hundred twelve, four thousand ninety six. Double n, and the work becomes eight times."),
(R,"So Gaussian elimination is also cubic?"),
(S,"Yes, about n cubed over three. But solving a triangular system costs only n squared. So factor once with L U, and every new right hand side costs just n squared."),
(S,"For n equals one thousand and a hundred right hand sides, redoing elimination each time costs thirty three billion operations. L U costs four hundred thirty three million. About seventy seven times cheaper."),
(N,"D S A twist. To find only the top eigenvalue, power iteration costs n squared per step, far below the n cubed of a full decomposition. Choose the algorithm by what you need.")],
"end":[(N,"The top of the tower. Vector spaces, matrix types, linear systems, rank, eigenvalues, quadratic forms, L U and S V D."),
(R,"So linear algebra is just geometry with a staircase and a mirror."),
(S,"And the exam is yours. Next film, calculus and optimization. See you there.")],
}
